<p><a name="fn7"></a>7. See Laubach and Williams (2015). For the most up-to-date Laubach-Williams estimates of the natural rate of interest, a concept closely related to the neutral rate, see <a href="http://www.frbsf.org/economic-research/files/Laubach_Williams_updated_estimates.xlsx">www.frbsf.org/economic-research/files/Laubach_Williams_updated_estimates.xlsx</a><cite>. </cite>Also see the most recent median estimates of the real neutral rate from the<cite> </cite>New York Federal Reserve Bank's Survey of Primary Dealers (<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/primarydealer_survey_questions.html">https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/primarydealer_survey_questions.html</a>) and Survey of Market Participants (<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/survey_market_participants.html">https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/survey_market_participants.html</a>). For estimates of the real neutral rate based on the Summary of Economic Projections, see Bongard and Johannsen (2016). <a href="#f7">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn8"></a>8. For an analysis of macroeconomic behavior near the zero lower bound, see Evans and others (2015). <a href="#f8">Return to text</a></p>

